# ==============================================================================
# 🛑 Evidence safety — deny by default
# ==============================================================================
#
# data_store/ holds real forensic evidence. This file denies everything and
# re-includes only the directory skeleton and documentation.
#
# It used to be an extension blocklist. A blocklist fails silently on any format
# nobody thought to add, and that had already happened: VMware exports
# (.vmdk, -flat.vmdk, .vmx, .ovf, .ova, .vmsd, .vmxf) were fully committable,
# because VM support was added to the pipeline without updating the list —
# and data_store/raw/VM_files/ is the documented place to put them.
#
# Deny-by-default means the next format nobody thought of is covered too.
#
# This is a safety net, not a guarantee. Check `git status` before committing.

# Ignore everything under data_store/ ...
*

# ... but descend into subdirectories, so the skeleton is preserved ...
!*/

# ... except fetched tool caches under dependencies/ (not committed — provisioned
# by --fetch). hayabusa ships an embedded git repo under rules/, which git would
# otherwise offer to add as a submodule.
/dependencies/hayabusa/

# ... and keep only these.
#
# This file only (anchored) — NOT a bare `!.gitignore`, which un-ignores every
# nested .gitignore in the evidence (imaged filesystems carry hundreds).
!/.gitignore
# Empty .gitkeep placeholders (empty by convention, so harmless even if one turns
# up in evidence).
!.gitkeep

# Documentation READMEs — anchored to their EXACT paths (leading `/`), NOT by name.
# A bare `!README.md` un-ignores every README.md in the tree, including ones that
# are part of the forensic evidence (iOS/Android app bundles, test corpora, …) and
# may carry secrets. Only these three skeleton READMEs are re-included.
!/README.md
!/dependencies/evtxecmd/README.md
!/processed/velociraptor/README.md

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Removed: !dependencies/SuperMem/**
#
# That rule was carried over from the old blocklist without checking whether it
# still applied. It does not: SuperMem's files were deleted from the repository
# in dc58d8c (2025-09-10) and nothing has been tracked under that path since.
#
# Leaving it was an unbounded `**` hole in a deny-by-default file, aimed at a
# directory for a MEMORY FORENSICS tool — so anything a future operator dropped
# or generated there, including memory-derived output, would have been
# committable. Exactly the failure mode this file was rewritten to close.
#
# If SuperMem is ever vendored again, add a narrow rule for the specific files
# and record it in THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
